//  04 · a pipeline that breaks in the middle — SOLUTION      ★★★ stretch
//  run: node 04-pipeline-error-cleanup.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  Walkthrough: a Transform reports failure through its callback —
//  `callback(err)` — not by throwing past it and not by emitting 'error'
//  by hand. That single call destroys the transform with the error, and
//  pipeline() propagates it: every other stream in the chain is destroyed
//  and the returned promise rejects with the original error, message
//  intact. That propagation is the whole reason to use pipeline() instead
//  of `a.pipe(b).pipe(c)`, where the source would sit there open, holding
//  a file handle or a socket, forever.
//  Note what the destroy log proves: the source is destroyed on the sad
//  path AND on the happy path (with no error), because streams are
//  autoDestroy by default. Cleanup is not a special case.
//  The partial output is the lesson people miss. 'A' and 'B' had already
//  been handed to the sink, so they are in the array — but anything still
//  sitting in a buffer when the error hit is thrown away with the stream.
//  A half-finished pipeline leaves you with a prefix of the work and no
//  way to know how long that prefix is. If the sink writes somewhere real,
//  that is a half-written file, which is why module 19's atomic write and
//  this exercise belong to the same story.
//  Wrong turn: `try { … } catch` INSIDE the transform to "keep going".
//  Now the pipeline resolves successfully having silently dropped
//  records, and nobody upstream ever learns the data is incomplete.

